But wait, there's more...

Readers will recall a certain $40 billion bridge loan SoftBank lined up in March to fund its latest $30 billion check to OpenAI. The good news is that it's gone: SoftBank drew $30 billion, repaid all of it by Sept 15 (six months early) and cancelled the rest. The bad news is how.

The bridge was taken out with:

- a new $11.87 billion two-year term loan;
- the $7.35 billion Intel collar above;
- another $5 billion on the margin loan backed by Arm shares, which now stands at ¥3.9 trillion (about $25 billion), secured by 769 million Arm shares and extended to 2028;
- and, for the rest, presumably some of the cash from its bond-selling spree, which included the largest junk bond in history.

In other words, a one-year loan was replaced with a two-year loan, a pawned chip stock, a bigger margin loan and more junk bonds.

